<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Derbent</h1><p class="st-meta">869 CE – 1075 CE (approx.) · capital Derbent</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab frontier emirate at the great Caspian gate of Derbent, guarding Islam's northern border against the Khazars and mountain peoples.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, called Bab al-Abwab, the Gate of Gates, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, had been fortified by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> with walls running from the Caspian into the mountains. In 869, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> authority faded, the local Arab governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashim-ibn-suraqa">Hashim ibn Suraqa</a> founded a hereditary dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashimids-of-derbent">Hashimids</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was a frontier city-state that drew warriors for holy war against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> and the peoples of the mountains, and grew rich on Caspian and caravan trade in slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and textiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emirs</a> shared power with an assembly of leading citizens and often fought or allied with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State</a>, which at times ruled the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by quarrels between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> and townsmen and by raids from the mountains, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was taken by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> in 1075. The city later passed among regional powers, keeping its walls as the gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-derbent.jpg" alt="Emirate of Derbent" width="320" height="208"><figcaption>Image: Armenica.org · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shaddadid.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>869 CE – 1075 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Derbent</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hashim ibn Suraqa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic; Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hashimid Emirate, Bab al-Abwab</dd></dl></aside></div>
